# First-Time Setup Guide

These instructions apply to ANY AI assistant working in this repo: Cursor, VSCode with the Claude Code extension or Copilot, the Claude desktop app, or the Claude Code CLI.

## Assume the user cannot use a terminal

Most Design Core users are designers, not developers. Treat every command in this file as something YOU run, never something you tell the user to run:

- **Run every command yourself** (`npm install`, `npm run doctor`, `npm run dev`, `gh ...`, all of them). Never say "run this in your terminal" or paste commands for the user to type.
- **When a step needs clicks you cannot do** (Publish Branch, Git: Clone, GitHub settings pages, accepting an invite, signing in), give exact click-by-click instructions, ONE step at a time, and wait for the user to confirm before the next one.
- **Narrate simply.** One plain sentence about what you are doing and why ("I'm starting the preview so you can see the tool"). No jargon, no git terminology unless the user uses it first.

## Step zero: verify before you act

At the start of any setup conversation (and any time something seems off), run:

```
npm run doctor
```

It prints, in plain language, which kind of repo this is (public template, company repo mid-setup, finished company repo, or misconfigured), what is broken, and the exact fix for each problem. **Fix what doctor reports before continuing. Never proceed with setup on a misconfigured repo.** If `npm install` has not run yet, run it first.

## When to trigger setup

**If doctor says this is the core repo** (the public template, `ubrandon/design-core`): the user is a developer maintaining the tool. **Do not run this setup flow** -- skip it entirely and follow `design-tool.mdc` in core repo mode.

Otherwise, check if the file `.designer` exists in the repo root. If it **does not exist**, the user has not completed setup yet. When they ask to "set up" or "get started" or paste a getting-started prompt, walk them through the right flow below.

If `.designer` already exists, setup is done -- skip this entirely and follow the normal `design-tool.mdc` rules.

## Detecting setup type

Doctor's "Repo:" line tells you which flow applies:

- **"a company repo mid-setup"** or **"the public Design Core template"** (after the user pasted the company prompt) → **Company setup** (first person at the org). They also say things like "setting up for my company" or "first time".
- **"a company repo (org/repo)"** with no `.designer` → **Designer joining** (the company repo already exists; this person just cloned it). They say things like "joining my team".

If genuinely unclear, ask: "Are you setting this up for your company for the first time, or joining a team that already has it?"

## Working outside Cursor

Where these rules mention Cursor-specific UI, use the equivalent:

- **"Open the AI chat"** -- Cursor: Cmd+L / Ctrl+L. VSCode: the Claude Code or Copilot panel. Claude desktop app: the conversation you are already in.
- **"Source Control panel / Publish Branch"** -- exists in Cursor and VSCode (the branch icon in the sidebar). In the Claude desktop app or CLI there is no panel; use the GitHub CLI flow described in "Publishing without an editor panel" below.
- **Cursor settings** (Agent mode on, model set to Auto) apply only in Cursor. In other clients there is nothing to configure.

