## Company setup flow (first person at the org)

Walk the user through conversationally. Ask one thing at a time. Be friendly and non-technical.

### 1. Verify the bootstrap

The getting-started prompt has the user clone over HTTPS, run `npm install`, then `npm run start-company-setup` (which disconnects the clone from the public template and blocks accidental pushes to it). Verify with `npm run doctor`:

- **"a company repo mid-setup"** -- bootstrap done, continue to step 2.
- **"the public Design Core template"** -- the bootstrap was skipped. Run `npm run start-company-setup` now. If it refuses because the folder is named `design-core`, the clone step was wrong; the folder should be `design-core-<company>`.
- Anything else -- follow doctor's fixes first.

Also confirm the basics exist: `git --version` and `node --version`. If git is missing on macOS: `xcode-select --install`. If Node is missing: download from [nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org) (or `brew install node` on a Mac). Node must be 18 or newer.

### 2. Collect company info

Ask, one at a time:

1. **"What's your company or team name?"** (e.g. `Acme Design`)
2. **"What's your GitHub organization or account name?"** (e.g. `acme-design`) -- where the new repo will live.

The repo will be named **`design-core-<company-slug>`** (lowercase, hyphenated). "Acme Design" → `design-core-acme-design`.

### 3. Collect identity and create `.designer`

Ask:

1. **"What's your name?"** -- display name for attribution on projects
2. **"Who else is on your design team?"** -- optional, they can add people later

Create `.designer` in the repo root (it is gitignored, never committed):

```json
{
  "name": "Their Name",
  "company": "Their Company",
  "team": [
    { "name": "Their Name" },
    { "name": "Teammate 1" }
  ]
}
```

Creating `.designer` now matters: `npm run finish-setup` reads the company name from it.

### 4. Publish the repo to GitHub

**In Cursor or VSCode:** stop and tell the user:

> "Your repo is ready. To connect it to GitHub:
> 1. Open the **Source Control** tab (the branch icon in the sidebar)
> 2. Click **Publish Branch**
> 3. Name it `design-core-COMPANY_SLUG` and choose **private**
>
> Let me know when that's done and I'll finish the setup."

Wait for confirmation. Do NOT try to create the repo via the terminal in these editors -- the editor's Git integration handles GitHub authentication cleanly.

**Publishing without an editor panel (Claude desktop app or CLI):** use the GitHub CLI:

1. Check for it: `gh --version`. If missing, install it (macOS: `brew install gh`; otherwise [cli.github.com](https://cli.github.com)).
2. Check sign-in: `gh auth status`. If not signed in, run `gh auth login --web` -- the user just types a short code into their browser. Never ask the user to create or paste tokens.
3. Create and push: `gh repo create ORG/design-core-COMPANY_SLUG --private --source=. --remote=origin --push`

If `gh` cannot be installed, fallback: have the user create an empty **private** repo named `design-core-COMPANY_SLUG` on github.com (walk them through the clicks), then run `git remote add origin <their repo's HTTPS URL>` and `git push -u origin main`.

### 5. Run finish-setup

After the repo is on GitHub, run:

```
npm run finish-setup
```

It automatically: rewrites `GETTING_STARTED.md` so it contains only joining instructions with the company's real repo link (teammates can no longer follow the wrong prompt), fills `.designer.example` with the company name, wires the read-only upstream connection for future tool updates, and sets the public share-link URL. Do not make these edits by hand.

Then ask the user once: "Ready for me to commit and push the setup files?" On yes, commit `GETTING_STARTED.md`, `.designer.example`, and `public/data/site.json` and push.

### 6. Enable GitHub Pages (optional, for shareable links)

Tell the user:

> "If you want shareable prototype links, enable GitHub Pages: on your repo's GitHub page go to **Settings → Pages → Source → GitHub Actions**. After the next push, prototypes will be live at `https://THEIR_ORG.github.io/design-core-COMPANY_SLUG/`."

### 7. Start the preview server

Run `npm run dev` and tell them to open **http://localhost:3000**. Explain what they'll see: the home page with a project hub.

### 8. Next steps

> "You're all set! A few things you might want to do:
> - **Create your first project** -- just tell me a name and I'll set it up
> - **Invite your team** -- two parts: (1) on GitHub, invite each designer to the repo with **write** access (repo **Settings → Collaborators**) -- without this they can't open or save work; (2) send them the joining guide: `https://github.com/THEIR_ORG/THEIR_REPO/blob/main/GETTING_STARTED.md`
> - **Explore the design system** -- click Design System in the nav
>
> What would you like to do first?"

