## Quick reference (show after next-steps)

> **Commands**
> - `npm run dev` -- start the preview server (http://localhost:3000)
> - `npm run doctor` -- check the repo's health and get plain-language fixes
> - `npm run update-tool` -- pull the latest tool improvements (any teammate can run it)
>
> **Prompts you can try**
> - "Create a project called ___"
> - "Create 5 screen designs for the project ___"
> - "Create a prototype for ___"
> - "Add to the design system from these screenshots" (attach images)

## Getting tool updates later

When the user asks for updates, run `npm run update-tool`. It connects the read-only upstream link if missing (so updating is not tied to one person's computer), shows what's incoming, merges, keeps the company's own files when they conflict, and reinstalls dependencies. After it finishes, remind the user to push so the team gets the update. Details: `docs/UPDATING.md`. Never hand-run `git merge upstream/main`; use the script.

## Important notes

- `.designer` is gitignored -- each person creates their own. Do NOT commit it.
- `.designer.example` IS committed -- `finish-setup` fills it with the company name so new designers get it pre-filled.
- `GETTING_STARTED.md` in a company repo is generated by `finish-setup` -- do not edit it by hand.
- In Cursor, remind the user once: Agent mode on (default), model set to **Auto**.

## Tool updates belong in the core repo ONLY

**NEVER make tool-level changes in a company repo.** Company repos receive tool updates via `npm run update-tool` (which merges from the upstream core repo, `ubrandon/design-core`). If you edit tool files (rule files, root HTML pages, scripts, styles, config, docs) directly in a company repo, those changes will conflict with future updates and can break the tool for the whole team. `npm run doctor` detects this drift.

The only tool-level files that change in a company repo are `GETTING_STARTED.md` and `.designer.example`, and `npm run finish-setup` writes those automatically during setup. After that, all tool changes flow from the core repo downstream.
